1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,369
Here's how to reverse engineer BambooHR from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,369 --> 00:00:09,200
Claude passed all twenty checks, but my spec never said not to email the new hire whose offer fell through.

3
00:00:09,983 --> 00:00:12,053
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:12,073 --> 00:00:16,362
It copies the part of BambooHR a small team needs so onboarding stops slipping.

5
00:00:16,511 --> 00:00:19,114
A template turns a start date into dated tasks.

6
00:00:19,157 --> 00:00:24,790
Each task has one owner: the manager, IT, the people team, a buddy, or the new hire.

7
00:00:24,917 --> 00:00:28,723
Late tasks are flagged, and everyone gets one email a day with what's theirs.

8
00:00:29,683 --> 00:00:31,518
The core rule is due dates.

9
00:00:31,603 --> 00:00:35,230
Every task is due a set number of days before or after the start date.

10
00:00:35,272 --> 00:00:37,875
If that lands on a weekend, it moves to the Friday before.

11
00:00:38,045 --> 00:00:42,348
So for a Monday start, the desk task, one day before, is due on Friday.

12
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:45,741
And when the start date moves, every open task moves with it.

13
00:00:46,683 --> 00:00:49,414
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

14
00:00:49,541 --> 00:00:52,358
The foundation, with templates, people and sign-in.

15
00:00:52,357 --> 00:00:54,065
Onboardings and tasks.

16
00:00:54,149 --> 00:00:55,430
The new hire's view.

17
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:58,370
Then the daily job, the digests and the export.

18
00:00:58,365 --> 00:01:03,124
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

19
00:01:04,083 --> 00:01:05,044
Here's what it built.

20
00:01:05,150 --> 00:01:11,209
Priya Nair's onboarding shows her manager, her buddy, and Ben's laptop task, due a week before she starts.

21
00:01:11,422 --> 00:01:14,552
The onboardings list shows Leo Grant with two tasks overdue.

22
00:01:14,523 --> 00:01:19,899
Creating one for Rosa warns that four tasks are already overdue, because her start date is close.

23
00:01:20,027 --> 00:01:22,182
Ben's tasks are grouped by when they're due.

24
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:26,140
And Leo's first weeks shows what others are doing for him, by first name only.

25
00:01:27,083 --> 00:01:33,427
In one final run, on a fresh database, all twenty checks passed, along with one hundred and nine automated tests.

26
00:01:34,350 --> 00:01:36,463
But the more useful result is what broke.

27
00:01:36,612 --> 00:01:38,916
The build exposed forty-four gaps in the spec.

28
00:01:38,916 --> 00:01:40,602
Places where Claude had to guess.

29
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:45,595
Check-in notes were private, yet any role could own a check-in, so the wrong person could end up reading one.

30
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:48,455
Nothing stopped the people team from deactivating themselves.

31
00:01:48,562 --> 00:01:52,936
And a task title starting with an equals sign could run as a formula in the export.

32
00:01:53,084 --> 00:01:55,191
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

33
00:01:56,117 --> 00:01:58,763
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

34
00:01:58,890 --> 00:02:05,995
We didn't test production sign-in, real email delivery, the hourly job on a real scheduler, other time zones, or hosting.

35
00:02:06,058 --> 00:02:08,416
Those are yours to check before your team relies on it.

36
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:10,822
So is it worth building?

37
00:02:11,056 --> 00:02:16,284
BambooHR charges a flat two hundred and fifty dollars a month for Core, up to twenty-five employees.

38
00:02:16,347 --> 00:02:20,420
Above that, it starts at ten dollars and twenty-five cents per employee per month.

39
00:02:20,524 --> 00:02:22,530
But that buys a whole HR system.

40
00:02:22,743 --> 00:02:26,690
If you need records, time off, payroll and benefits, buy it.

41
00:02:26,711 --> 00:02:29,405
If onboarding is the part you need, build it.

42
00:02:30,350 --> 00:02:31,823
Everything is in one file.

43
00:02:31,929 --> 00:02:38,571
The data model, thirteen rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all twenty checks, with the forty-four fixes already in.

44
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:42,791
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

45
00:02:42,791 --> 00:02:44,921
If you build it, tell me what broke.

46
00:02:44,921 --> 00:02:45,394
I'm Sam.

47
00:02:45,394 --> 00:02:46,400
See you tomorrow.

